    "result_text": "Green. Done.\n\n**What I changed (agreeing with Finding 1):**\n\nThe \"prefer structured, else flat\" selection rule was open-coded in both `NormalizeOwnerAddress` and `ApplyOwner` \u2014 exactly the kind of single rule this package centralizes (`AddressNormalizer` \"the single definition of address normalization\", `OwnerHousehold`, `EnsurePostcodeValidForCity`). I hoisted it into one place:\n\n- **`AddressNormalizer.effectiveAddress(line1, line2, flat)`** \u2014 now the single home of *\"line1 non-blank ? compose(line1,line2) : flat\"*, sitting alongside `compose`/`normalize`. Each argument is used as given, so callers pass normalized or raw values as their step requires.\n- **`NormalizeOwnerAddress`** (create) normalizes each field, sets the structured fields back, then delegates the selection to `effectiveAddress` and keeps its own blank-check/400.\n- **`ApplyOwner`** (update) copies the request fields onto the entity and calls `effectiveAddress` for the stored flat address \u2014 no more duplicated branch.\n- I also made **`compose`** treat a blank `line2` as absent (`isBlank`), so the primitive behaves the same whether fed normalized or raw lines, removing the `isEmpty`/`isBlank` inconsistency the review flagged.\n\nA maintainer changing the preference/composition rule now edits one unit.\n\n**On the scope note (left as-is, intentionally):** the update pipeline has no `NormalizeOwnerAddress` step, so `ApplyOwner` still stores raw structured lines while create stores normalized ones. That pre-existing behavioural difference (PUT never normalized the flat address either) is a separate question the review explicitly set aside; centralizing the selection rule is precisely what keeps that a one-line behavioural decision if it's ever revisited. Acceptance behaviour is unchanged \u2014 full suite green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
