    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe `compose` primitive is well-placed on `AddressNormalizer` and shared by both callers \u2014 that part is clean and idiomatic. But the *address-selection rule* built on top of it is duplicated, which is out of step with how this codebase centralizes rules.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 The \"prefer structured, else flat\" decision is written twice, in two units that have already drifted.**\n\nThe business rule *\"when `addressLine1` is non-blank, the effective flat address is `compose(line1, line2)`; otherwise use the flat `address`\"* now lives in both:\n\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress.service` (create path, `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java:27-42`)\n- `ApplyOwner.service` (update path, `ApplyOwner.java:12-20`)\n\nThis is the exact kind of single rule that the rest of this package deliberately keeps in one place \u2014 `AddressNormalizer` is documented as \"The single definition of address normalization,\" `OwnerHousehold` as \"The single definition of household identity,\" `EnsurePostcodeValidForCity` as the \"single shared source of the range fact.\" The address-selection rule should follow the same idiom rather than being open-coded in two functions.\n\nThe duplication has *already* caused divergence, which is the concrete hazard:\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` composes from the *normalized* lines and null-normalizes a blank line2.\n- `ApplyOwner` composes from the *raw* request lines (`request.getAddressLine1()`/`getAddressLine2()` straight through) with no normalization and a different blankness check (`isBlank()` vs `isEmpty()`).\n\nA maintainer changing the composition/preference rule (e.g. a comma separator, or a different blank definition) must now find and edit both copies and reconcile their subtle differences. That's precisely the maintenance trap the \"single definition\" pattern here exists to avoid.\n\n**Suggested direction:** hoist the selection into one helper, e.g. `AddressNormalizer.effectiveAddress(line1, line2, flat)` that encapsulates the \"line1 non-blank ? compose(line1,line2) : flat\" branch, and have both `NormalizeOwnerAddress` and `ApplyOwner` call it. That keeps the preference rule in the same unit as `compose`/`normalize`, and leaves each step to do only its own job (normalization vs. copying request fields onto the entity).\n\n(Scope note, not part of the finding: the update pipeline has no `NormalizeOwnerAddress` step, so `ApplyOwner` stores raw structured lines while create stores normalized ones. Whether the two paths *should* normalize alike is a behavioural question I'm leaving aside \u2014 but it reinforces why the shared rule belongs in one place.)",
